```java
// Java 17 + Spring Boot 3.2 — jakarta.persistence
import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.util.*;

// OneToOne: User has one Profile
@Entity
class User {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String username;

    @OneToOne(cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    @JoinColumn(name = "profile_id") // owning side: holds the FK
    private Profile profile;
}

// OneToMany / ManyToOne: Department has many Employees
@Entity
class Department {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String name;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "department") // inverse side
    private List<Employee> employees = new ArrayList<>();
}

@Entity
class Employee {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String name;

    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "department_id") // owning side: holds the FK
    private Department department;
}

// ManyToMany: Student enrolls in many Courses
@Entity
class Student {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String name;

    @ManyToMany
    @JoinTable(name = "student_course",
        joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "student_id"),
        inverseJoinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "course_id"))
    private Set<Course> courses = new HashSet<>();
}

@Entity
class Course {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String title;

    @ManyToMany(mappedBy = "courses") // inverse side
    private Set<Student> students = new HashSet<>();
}
```
The `@JoinColumn`/`@JoinTable` side always owns the relationship; the `mappedBy` side is read-only navigation.

```java
// WRONG: bidirectional pair, but @OneToMany has no mappedBy
@Entity
class Department {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    @OneToMany // no mappedBy -> Hibernate adds an EXTRA join table
    private List<Employee> employees = new ArrayList<>();
}

@Entity
class Employee {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "department_id") // this FK already models the link
    private Department department;
}
// Result: employee.department_id AND a department_employees join table.
```
One logical link ends up stored twice, so inserts and updates fight each other.

```java
// RIGHT: mappedBy says the FK already lives on Employee
@Entity
class Department {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "department") // inverse side, no new column
    private List<Employee> employees = new ArrayList<>();
}

@Entity
class Employee {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "department_id") // owning side: the single FK
    private Department department;
}
// Result: one clean department_id column, no extra table.
```

Without mappedBy, two annotations can describe two independent unidirectional relationships. Depending on the mapping, that can lead to an unwanted join table or another relationship representation. mappedBy joins the two Java navigation paths into one bidirectional association.

~~~java
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "order")
private List<OrderItem> items = new ArrayList<>();

// in OrderItem
@ManyToOne
@JoinColumn(name = "order_id")
private Order order;
~~~

The inverse side is not a second owner. For a one-to-one, mappedBy works the same way. For many-to-many, one side defines @JoinTable and the other names the owning collection with mappedBy.

```java
@Entity
class Order {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String orderNumber;

    // Inverse side: mappedBy points to the 'order' field in OrderItem
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<OrderItem> items = new ArrayList<>();

    // Helper keeps BOTH sides in sync in memory
    public void addItem(OrderItem item) {
        items.add(item);
        item.setOrder(this); // set the owning side — this is what persists
    }
}

@Entity
class OrderItem {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String productName;
    private int quantity;

    // Owning side: has @JoinColumn, controls the FK column
    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "order_id", nullable = false)
    private Order order;

    public void setOrder(Order order) { this.order = order; }
}
// Without mappedBy, Hibernate would create BOTH:
//   1. order_item.order_id (from @ManyToOne)
//   2. an order_items join table (from @OneToMany)
```

