```java
// Composition: OrderItem belongs entirely to Order -> cascade ALL + orphanRemoval
@Entity
class Order {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order",
               cascade = CascadeType.ALL,   // save/update/delete propagate
               orphanRemoval = true)        // removing from list deletes the row
    private List<OrderItem> items = new ArrayList<>();
}

// Association: Employee outlives the Department -> NO cascade REMOVE
@Entity
class Department {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "department",
               cascade = { CascadeType.PERSIST, CascadeType.MERGE }) // never REMOVE
    private List<Employee> employees = new ArrayList<>();
}

// DANGER: cascade REMOVE from child to parent deletes the shared parent!
@Entity
class BadEmployee {
    @ManyToOne(cascade = CascadeType.REMOVE) // deleting one employee deletes the dept
    @JoinColumn(name = "department_id")
    private Department department;
}
```
Match the cascade to ownership: full cascade for owned children, persist/merge for associations, never REMOVE from child to parent.

~~~java
@ManyToMany
@JoinTable(name = "student_course",
    joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "student_id"),
    inverseJoinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "course_id"))
private Set<Course> courses = new HashSet<>();

// in Course
@ManyToMany(mappedBy = "courses")
private Set<Student> students = new HashSet<>();
~~~

Add a database unique constraint across both foreign keys if duplicate links are not allowed. Avoid CascadeType.REMOVE when the other entity is shared: removing one Student should normally remove join rows, not delete Course records. Large many-to-many collections can also be difficult to page and update, which is another reason a link entity is often clearer.

```java
@Entity
class Student {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String name;

    // Owning side: defines the join table
    @ManyToMany
    @JoinTable(name = "student_course",
        joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "student_id"),
        inverseJoinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "course_id"))
    private Set<Course> courses = new HashSet<>(); // Set, not List

    public void enroll(Course course) {
        courses.add(course);
        course.getStudents().add(this); // keep both sides in sync
    }
}

@Entity
class Course {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String title;

    @ManyToMany(mappedBy = "courses") // inverse side
    private Set<Student> students = new HashSet<>();
    public Set<Student> getStudents() { return students; }
}

// When the link needs its own data, use a junction entity instead:
@Entity
class Enrollment {
    @EmbeddedId private EnrollmentId id;

    @ManyToOne @MapsId("studentId") private Student student;
    @ManyToOne @MapsId("courseId") private Course course;

    private LocalDate enrollmentDate; // extra data a join table cannot hold
    private String grade;
}

@Embeddable
record EnrollmentId(Long studentId, Long courseId) {}
```

