```java
// Order knows its Customer; Customer has no orders list
@Entity
class Order {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "customer_id") // owning side, the only side
    private Customer customer;
}

@Entity
class Customer {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String name;
    // no List<Order> — you can't navigate customer -> orders in Java
}
// No mappedBy, no sync code, no JSON loop. Simplest possible mapping.
```

```java
@Entity
class Order {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "customer_id") // owning side
    @JsonBackReference                // breaks the JSON infinite loop
    private Customer customer;
}

@Entity
class Customer {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String name;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "customer") // inverse side
    @JsonManagedReference             // serialized; the back side is skipped
    private List<Order> orders = new ArrayList<>();

    // Helper keeps both sides consistent in memory
    public void addOrder(Order order) {
        orders.add(order);
        order.setCustomer(this);
    }
}
```
Bidirectional buys two-way navigation but costs mappedBy, sync helpers, and JSON-loop handling.

The word orphan describes a child that is no longer referenced by its owning relationship. JPA detects that relationship change while the entities are managed and performs the delete at flush.

~~~java
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "order",
    cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
private List<OrderItem> items = new ArrayList<>();

void removeItem(OrderItem item) {
    items.remove(item);
    item.setOrder(null);
}
~~~

Database ON DELETE CASCADE is a separate database feature. Bulk JPQL deletes also bypass normal per-entity cascade and lifecycle behavior, so do not assume these annotations govern every deletion path.

```java
@Entity
class BlogPost {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String title;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "post",
               cascade = CascadeType.ALL, // delete post -> delete comments
               orphanRemoval = true)      // remove from list -> delete comment
    private List<Comment> comments = new ArrayList<>();

    public void removeComment(Comment c) {
        comments.remove(c); // orphanRemoval fires on flush
        c.setPost(null);
    }
    public List<Comment> getComments() { return comments; }
}

@Entity
class Comment {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String text;

    @ManyToOne @JoinColumn(name = "post_id")
    private BlogPost post;
    public void setPost(BlogPost post) { this.post = post; }
}

// Scenario A: parent survives, child is orphaned -> orphanRemoval deletes it
var post = postRepository.findById(1L).orElseThrow();
post.removeComment(post.getComments().get(0));
postRepository.save(post); // DELETE issued for that one comment

// Scenario B: parent deleted -> CascadeType.REMOVE deletes every comment
postRepository.delete(post);
```
